  "id": "Compound::DB01223 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Aminophylline is implicated in the pathogenesis of Asthma through a series of interconnected mechanisms involving muscarinic receptor activation and bronchodilation. The evidence suggests that Aminophylline causes Tremor Muscle, which is then influenced by different bronchodilators—Formoterol, Arformoterol, and Salbutamol—each of which is known to treat Asthma. These bronchodilators are chemically related, sharing a common structure and function in relaxing airway smooth muscle. The shared pathway from Aminophylline to Asthma through these bronchodilators highlights a potential mechanism by which Aminophylline may influence airway function, possibly through a broader class of drugs that act on similar receptors or pathways. The integration of these relationships underscores the role of Aminophylline in modulating bronchodilator effects, suggesting a possible link between Aminophylline and Asthma through its influence on muscarinic receptor activity and airway smooth muscle regulation.",
